+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: `postcard`
+
+Property-based tests were added to the `postcard` crate (`source/postcard`)
+using [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` dev-dependency). Tests live in
+the existing integration test files:
+
+- `source/postcard/tests/loopback.rs` (module `hegel_props`, 24 tests)
+- `source/postcard/tests/accumulator.rs` (module `hegel_props`, 1 test)
+
+All properties are grounded in the postcard wire-format specification
+(`spec/src/wire-format.md`) and the documented contracts of the public API.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+1. **Rich derived-type round-trip** (`prop_rich_message_roundtrip`):
+   `from_bytes(to_slice(x)) == x` for a `RichMessage` struct exercising
+   nested enums (unit / newtype / tuple / struct variants), `Option`,
+   `heapless::Vec`/`String` collections, full-unicode strings and chars,
+   floats, byte arrays, and full-range integers (`u64`, `u128`, `i128`,
+   `i32`, `i8`, `usize`, ...).
+
+2. **Varint round-trip + spec maximum encoded length** (12 tests via
+   `varint_roundtrip_props!`, one per type `u8`–`u128`, `usize`, `i8`–`i128`,
+   `isize`): every value over the type's full range round-trips through
+   `to_slice`/`take_from_bytes`, decoding consumes exactly the encoding
+   (empty remainder), and the encoded length never exceeds the spec's
+   "Maximum Encoded Length" table (1/3/5/10/19 bytes; `usize`/`isize`
+   maxima assume the 64-bit test platform, per the spec's `usize` mapping).
+
+3. **Spec-conformant unsigned varint encoding**
+   (`prop_unsigned_varint_encoding_matches_leb128_reference`): encoded bytes
+   for `u16`/`u32`/`u64`/`u128`/`usize` equal an independent canonical
+   LEB128 reference implementation written from the spec.
+
+4. **Spec-conformant signed varint encoding**
+   (`prop_signed_varint_encoding_matches_zigzag_leb128_reference`): encoded
+   bytes for `i16`/`i32`/`i64`/`i128`/`isize` equal
+   `leb128(zigzag(n))` per the spec's "Signed Integer Encoding" section
+   (zigzag is numerically width-independent, so the i128-width reference is
+   valid for all narrower types).
+
+5. **Non-canonical varint acceptance/rejection**
+   (`prop_varint_noncanonical_padding_accepted_within_max_len`): per the
+   spec's "Canonicalization" table, an encoding padded with excess `0x80`
+   continuation bytes is accepted (decoding to the same `u32`) while total
+   length stays within 5 bytes, and rejected with
+   `Error::DeserializeBadVarint` once it exceeds it.
+
+6. **`serialized_size` consistency** (`prop_serialized_size_matches_to_slice`):
+   `experimental::serialized_size(&x)` equals the length `to_slice` actually
+   writes.
+
+7. **Buffer sizing behavior** (`prop_to_slice_exact_buffer_ok_undersized_errors`):
+   `to_slice` succeeds into an exactly-sized buffer and returns
+   `Err(Error::SerializeBufferFull)` — never panics or overruns — for every
+   undersized buffer length (0..n drawn).
+
+8. **Exact remainder** (`prop_take_from_bytes_returns_exact_remainder`):
+   `take_from_bytes` on `encoding ++ arbitrary_suffix` returns the original
+   value and exactly `arbitrary_suffix` as the unconsumed remainder.
+
+9. **Truncation rejection** (`prop_from_bytes_rejects_truncated_input`):
+   every strict prefix of a valid encoding fails to decode (the parser is
+   deterministic, so it must hit unexpected end-of-input).
+
+10. **Decode robustness** (`prop_decoders_never_panic_on_arbitrary_input`):
+    `from_bytes`, `take_from_bytes`, and `from_bytes_cobs` never panic on
+    arbitrary byte input (up to 512 bytes) for the rich message type.
+
+11. **COBS flavor round-trip + framing guarantee**
+    (`prop_cobs_roundtrip_and_zero_free_framing`): `to_slice_cobs` output
+    ends with the documented `0x00` sentinel, contains no interior zero
+    bytes, and `from_bytes_cobs` round-trips the value.
+
+12. **Float bit-pattern round-trip** (`prop_float_bitpattern_roundtrip`):
+    per spec sections 12/13, `f32`/`f64` encode as exactly their
+    little-endian bit patterns and round-trip bit-exactly for *arbitrary*
+    bit patterns, including NaN payloads and infinities.
+
+13. **Fixint layout + round-trip** (`prop_fixint_layout_and_roundtrip`):
+    fields using `#[serde(with = "postcard::fixint::le/be")]` are laid out
+    as fixed-size little/big-endian byte arrays (oracle:
+    `to_le_bytes`/`to_be_bytes`) and round-trip.
+
+14. **Accumulator reassembly under arbitrary chunking**
+    (`prop_accumulator_reassembles_arbitrarily_chunked_stream`): feeding a
+    well-formed COBS stream of arbitrary messages to `CobsAccumulator` in
+    arbitrarily-sized chunks (mid-frame splits, multi-frame chunks)
+    reproduces exactly the original message sequence, with no `OverFull` or
+    `DeserError` results.
+
+## Test results
+
+- Run command (matches CI's feature set for this crate):
+  `cargo test -p postcard --features=alloc,experimental-derive,use-std,use-crc,nalgebra-v0_33,heapless-v0_8,heapless-v0_9`
+- **All 25 property tests pass**, alongside all pre-existing tests
+  (52 lib unit tests, 22 fixint/max_size/crc-style tests, etc.).
+- The integration-test subset also passes under default features
+  (`cargo test -p postcard --test loopback --test accumulator`).
+- Liveness was verified by temporarily inverting the rich round-trip
+  assertion: hegel reported and shrank a minimal counterexample
+  (`RichMessage { seq: 0, variant: Unit, maybe_more: None, text: "" }`),
+  then the change was reverted and the failure database removed.
+- An exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` on every property) passed with
+  no failures; committed tests use the default case count.
+- `cargo fmt --check` and
+  `cargo clippy -p postcard --tests <CI features> -- --deny=warnings` are
+  clean.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+No wire-format or API-contract bugs were found in `postcard` itself.
+
+One **pre-existing build failure** (not introduced by this work, present at
+HEAD) was confirmed while running the suite: `cargo test -p postcard` with
+*default* features fails to compile the crate's own unit tests with
+
+```
+error[E0432]: unresolved import `crate::max_size`
+   --> source/postcard/src/ser/mod.rs:501:16
+```
+
+The unit-test module at `source/postcard/src/ser/mod.rs:497` is gated only
+on `#[cfg(feature = "heapless")]` but imports `crate::max_size::MaxSize`,
+which exists only under the `experimental-derive` feature
+(`source/postcard/src/lib.rs:21-22`). CI never hits this because `ci.sh`
+always tests with `experimental-derive` enabled. It was left unfixed per the
+"do not fix bugs" ground rule.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **`PartialEq` round-trip for NaN floats**: `deserialize(serialize(NaN)) ==
+  NaN` is false under IEEE `==` even when the library is bit-exact. Instead
+  of weakening the rich-type property, NaN is excluded from the rich type's
+  float generator (with a comment), and a separate bit-pattern property
+  (`prop_float_bitpattern_roundtrip`) covers NaN payloads via
+  `to_bits`/`from_bits` comparison — strictly stronger than `==`.
+- **`leb128` reference for `u8`/`i8`**: initially considered including
+  `u8`/`i8` in the LEB128-oracle tests; abandoned as unsound because the
+  spec explicitly stores 8-bit types as raw bytes, not varints (a `u8 >=
+  128` would be a 2-byte LEB128 but a 1-byte postcard encoding). They remain
+  in the round-trip/max-length family with max length 1.
+
+No property failed during development for reasons other than the deliberate
+liveness check.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference was accurate throughout: `cargo add --dev hegeltest`
+  with the `hegel` crate name, `#[hegel::composite]`, plain
+  `&TestCase`-taking helper functions (needed here for const-generic
+  `heapless::String<N>` drawing, which composites can't express), and the
+  `one_of!` char idiom all worked first try.
+- The "verify the tests are live" and "10x exploratory run" steps were cheap
+  and valuable; the shrunk counterexample from the liveness check confirmed
+  draws were being recorded correctly.
+- One friction point: this workspace's CI enforces `clippy
+  --deny=warnings` on tests, and generated payload types tripped
+  `clippy::large_enum_variant` / test code tripped `clippy::same_item_push`.
+  A note in the reference about lint-strict projects (beyond the existing
+  `let_underscore_drop` mention) would save an iteration.
+- A documented way to draw a full-range `char` directly (rather than the
+  codepoint `one_of!` idiom) would slightly shorten unicode-heavy tests.
